<SiteHeader active="thoughts" />
  <main id="top"><section class="page-hero section-pad"><p class="eyebrow">RESEARCH NOTES</p><h1>Glacier change,<br /><em>in context.</em></h1><p>Short, accessible notes on questions Sarah Child’s research helps answer. Each note points to the original study and a longer explainer.</p></section>
    <section class="notebook section-pad" aria-label="Research notes">
      <div class="notebook-intro"><p class="eyebrow">FROM THE PUBLISHED RESEARCH</p><p>Short notes on the methods, observations, and findings behind the glacier studies.</p></div>
      <div class="notebook-grid">
        <article class="notebook-entry" id="long-record"><p class="notebook-meta">ALASKA · HISTORICAL RECORD · 2025</p><h2>What changes when the glacier record gets longer?</h2><p>Satellite records cover only part of a glacier’s history. For Kennicott and Root Glaciers, researchers combined photographs dating to 1938 with elevation and bed data to build an 85-year record. Their analysis found that adding this longer history changed projected mass loss by as much as 22% compared with estimates calibrated using only recent decades.</p><div class="notebook-links"><a href="/glacier-history.html">Read the research note <span>→</span></a><a href="https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC12373804/" target="_blank" rel="noreferrer">Read the full paper <span>↗</span></a></div></article>
        <article class="notebook-entry" id="old-photos"><p class="notebook-meta">EAST ANTARCTICA · PHOTOGRAMMETRY · 2021</p><h2>How can old photographs become elevation data?</h2><p>Overlapping airphotos contain clues about three-dimensional terrain. Using images of Byrd Glacier from 1978–79, Sarah Child and co-authors reconstructed a historical surface and compared it with newer elevation data. In this study, the glacier’s surface elevation remained roughly constant across the approximately 40-year comparison.</p><div class="notebook-links"><a href="/glacier-photogrammetry.html">Read the research note <span>→</span></a><a href="https://doi.org/10.3390/rs13010021" target="_blank" rel="noreferrer">Read the full paper <span>↗</span></a></div></article>
        <article class="notebook-entry" id="hidden-flood"><p class="notebook-meta">BYRD GLACIER · SUBGLACIAL FLOODING · 2021</p><h2>Can a crevasse reveal a flood beneath the ice?</h2><p>A flood under a glacier is hard to observe directly. At Byrd Glacier, a 2006 subglacial flood was followed by a temporary speed increase, while later radar measurements showed large fractures near the grounding line. The researchers linked the largest basal crevasse to the flood and explored how surface depressions can preserve clues to past events below.</p><div class="notebook-links"><a href="/byrd-crevasses.html">Read the research note <span>→</span></a><a href="https://doi.org/10.1029/2020GL090978" target="_blank" rel="noreferrer">Read the full paper <span>↗</span></a></div></article>
        <article class="notebook-entry" id="between-calvings"><p class="notebook-meta">GREENLAND · HELHEIM GLACIER · 2024</p><h2>What happens between iceberg calving events?</h2><p>Repeated laser scans at Helheim Glacier captured changes that occasional satellite images can miss. The study tracked surface depressions and glacier motion, and found that individual calving episodes during the observation period did not cause a lasting increase in glacier speed. It also documented a grounding-zone retreat of more than three kilometres between 2018 and 2019.</p><div class="notebook-links"><a href="/glacier-calving.html">Read the research note <span>→</span></a><a href="https://doi.org/10.1029/2024JF008059" target="_blank" rel="noreferrer">Read the full paper <span>↗</span></a></div></article>
        <article class="notebook-entry" id="grounding-zone"><p class="notebook-meta">GREENLAND · HELHEIM GLACIER · OBSERVATIONS</p><h2>How do researchers track where a glacier meets the ocean?</h2><p>The grounding zone is the transition where grounded ice begins to float. At Helheim Glacier, repeated laser scans, satellite imagery, and elevation measurements helped researchers follow this boundary. They documented more than three kilometres of retreat between 2018 and 2019, showing how quickly the contact zone can shift.</p><div class="notebook-links"><a href="/glacier-calving.html">Read the research note <span>→</span></a><a href="https://doi.org/10.1029/2024JF008059" target="_blank" rel="noreferrer">Read the full paper <span>↗</span></a></div></article>
        <article class="notebook-entry" id="surface-depressions"><p class="notebook-meta">GREENLAND · HELHEIM GLACIER · METHOD</p><h2>What can a dip in the glacier surface tell us?</h2><p>Researchers used repeated high-resolution laser scans to map surface depressions and track how they developed before calving. Comparing those measurements with satellite images, elevation, velocity, and strain data helped connect visible surface features with processes near the glacier bed.</p><div class="notebook-links"><a href="/glacier-calving.html">Read the research note <span>→</span></a><a href="https://doi.org/10.1029/2024JF008059" target="_blank" rel="noreferrer">Read the full paper <span>↗</span></a></div></article>
        <article class="notebook-entry" id="radar-under-ice"><p class="notebook-meta">BYRD GLACIER · EAST ANTARCTICA · METHOD</p><h2>How can radar reveal fractures at a glacier’s base?</h2><p>Airborne radar can detect structures hidden beneath the ice surface. At Byrd Glacier, radar observations identified hundreds of basal crevasses. Researchers combined those measurements with satellite feature tracking to estimate when large fractures formed and compare them with the timing of past subglacial flooding.</p><div class="notebook-links"><a href="/byrd-crevasses.html">Read the research note <span>→</span></a><a href="https://doi.org/10.1029/2020GL090978" target="_blank" rel="noreferrer">Read the full paper <span>↗</span></a></div></article>
